Implementation:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io
::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    if !old_path.exists() {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound,
            format!("note '{}' not found", old_id),
        ));
    }
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 || parts[0] != old_id {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            format!("note '{}' is corrupt or missing", old_id),
        ));
    }
    let (pinned_flag, body) = (parts[2], parts[3]);
    let new_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id));
    if new_id != old_id {
        if new_path.exists() {
            return Err(std::io::Error::new(
                std::io::ErrorKind::AlreadyExists,
                format!("note '{}' already exists", new_id),
            ));
        }
        let content = format!("{}{}{}{}{}{}{}", new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned
_flag, SEP, body);
        fs::write(&new_path, content)?;
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    } else {
        let content = format!("{}{}{}{}{}{}{}", new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned
_flag, SEP, body);
        fs::write(&old_path, content)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Should we validate `parts[0] == old_id`? The id is stored as the first field. If a
 file named `old_id.note` has a different first field, it's inconsistent. Treating
 that as InvalidData seems fine, but if the test creates a file directly with a mismatch
... unlikely. Actually, wait — should we trust the filename or the content? load_all
 trusts the content. To be lenient, it might be better to just require parts.len()
 == 4 without checking the id. If the content's id differs, the "no traces of the
 note under the old id" test: load_all uses the content's id, so it would end up reporting
 the content's id. Hmm. To match load_all's behavior, let's not check the id — load
_all doesn't check either. Actually, checking parts[0] == old_id adds robustness,
 but if the test creates a file via save() (which writes a consistent id) there's
 no problem. To match load_all's leniency, I'll skip the strict check, but keep the
 len==4 check.

